  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nSurface high will push eastward this morning. Before doing so\nsome colder temperatures will be found across the area, with\nsome isolated areas below freezing. Current isolated observations\ncombined with increasing clouds will keep confidence to low for\na freeze highlight, although some areas will see frost to \npatchy freeze this morning. As the high moves east today, a weak\ndisturbance aloft will lift the stalled front in the west and\nproduce some weak isentropic lift. This will bring increased\nclouds and perhaps a few very light rain showers or sprinkles\nfrom west to east today. Meanwhile an increased gradient at the\nsurface will bring some breezy southerly winds today. Slightly\nwarmer temperatures will be found in the west and the warmer\nside of the elevated front, with highs near 70 degrees.\nElsewhere will generally see highs in the 60s. Surface low then\nmoves through tonight through Saturday. This will also bring a\ntypical warm front followed by a cold front with this low. As a\nresult more scattered light rain showers or sprinkles are \npossible tonight through much of the day on Saturday. Confidence\nwas too low for slight pops, although did keep in sprinkle\nmentions. Behind the weak cold front will bring a switch to\na breezy northwest wind sometime Saturday. With drying\nconditions behind this front, some lower RH combined with these\nbreezy winds could bring some increased fire weather concern\nSaturday afternoon and early evening. This will mainly be\nconfined to the far southwest. Look for temperatures in the 60s\nto lower 70s as well on Saturday. Saturday night through Sunday\nthen sees a brief period of high pressure. This will lessen\nwinds, clear skies, and bring cooler overnight lows in the 30s\nto lower 40s Saturday night into Sunday morning. With this high\npressure remaining through Sunday, look for seasonable highs in\nthe 60s to near 70 along with light winds. \n\nFlat ridging aloft will provide for weak and mainly dry\ndisturbances to move across the area early to mid next week.\nChances for any precipitation are low right now with these\ndisturbances. Mainly concern will be some increased winds to\nstart the week combined with lower RH values especially in the\nsouthwest. This could return some fire weather concerns\nespecially in these southwestern areas early next week. Westerly\nflow aloft will also provide with a general warming trend for\nnext week with highs back in the 70s to perhaps some lower 80s.\nLows look to be above freezing and generally in the 40s and 50s.\nLarger temperature spreads are then starting to show up for\nlater next week as some clusters attempt to break down the\nridging pattern with another pacific north west trough. Clusters\nare still fairly split on this ridge breaking down thus the \ncurrent forecast maintains mainly dry conditions with above \nnormal temperatures for later next week.",
